1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,881
Welcome to the Modern Java Enterprise Handbook.

2
00:00:03,881 --> 00:00:06,744
This is chapter eighteen: list internals.

3
00:00:07,194 --> 00:00:10,817
List is the collection you reach for without thinking.

4
00:00:10,817 --> 00:00:14,640
That reflex is usually right, and occasionally expensive.

5
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:23,898
Let's look at how ArrayList really grows, why LinkedList loses benchmarks it should win on paper, and which list-like views will bite you.

6
00:00:24,798 --> 00:00:29,335
The distinction that predicts performance isn't array versus nodes.

7
00:00:29,335 --> 00:00:31,570
It's contiguous versus scattered.

8
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:39,561
An ArrayList keeps its element references in one block of memory, so walking it streams cleanly through the CPU cache.

9
00:00:40,011 --> 00:00:45,898
A LinkedList allocates a separate Node object for every element, scattered across the heap.

10
00:00:45,898 --> 00:00:47,904
Every step can be a cache miss.

11
00:00:48,354 --> 00:00:58,551
For a million elements, that's around five megabytes of overhead for ArrayList, and around twenty-four for LinkedList, spread over a million objects.

12
00:00:59,001 --> 00:01:03,945
So LinkedList loses even where the complexity table says it should win.

13
00:01:03,945 --> 00:01:10,073
Use ArrayList by default, ArrayDeque for queues and stacks, and LinkedList almost never.

14
00:01:10,973 --> 00:01:14,231
Inside, ArrayList is just an array and a size.

15
00:01:14,231 --> 00:01:18,622
A default ArrayList doesn't even allocate until the first add.

16
00:01:18,622 --> 00:01:23,084
It shares a static empty array, so empty lists are nearly free.

17
00:01:23,084 --> 00:01:26,696
When it fills up, it grows by one and a half times.

18
00:01:27,146 --> 00:01:31,912
So capacity walks ten, fifteen, twenty-two, thirty-three, and so on.

19
00:01:31,912 --> 00:01:38,431
One and a half wastes less memory than doubling, and lets the allocator reuse earlier blocks.

20
00:01:38,881 --> 00:01:43,772
Reaching a thousand elements takes twelve grow-and-copy operations.

21
00:01:43,772 --> 00:01:45,961
Sizing it up front takes zero.

22
00:01:45,961 --> 00:01:51,654
And a trap: passing zero to save memory gives the worst growth profile of all.

23
00:01:51,654 --> 00:01:53,260
Zero, one, two, three.

24
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:58,099
Inserting in the middle is O of n, but that's misleading.

25
00:01:58,099 --> 00:02:00,517
It's a single System dot arraycopy.

26
00:02:00,967 --> 00:02:06,087
And arraycopy is a JIT intrinsic, compiled to a vectorised memory move.

27
00:02:06,087 --> 00:02:15,389
Shifting ten thousand references takes microseconds, which often beats a LinkedList that first has to walk to the right position.

28
00:02:15,839 --> 00:02:20,436
Removal nulls out the vacated slot so the element can be collected.

29
00:02:20,436 --> 00:02:22,837
But the array itself never shrinks.

30
00:02:22,837 --> 00:02:28,532
A list that peaked at a million entries keeps that array until you call trimToSize.

31
00:02:28,982 --> 00:02:32,896
And every structural change increments a modCount field.

32
00:02:32,896 --> 00:02:39,186
Iterators compare it on each step and throw ConcurrentModificationException if it changed.

33
00:02:39,186 --> 00:02:42,681
That's best-effort bug detection, not a guarantee.

34
00:02:42,681 --> 00:02:48,971
Set doesn't count as structural, which is why replacing elements during iteration is fine.

35
00:02:49,871 --> 00:02:53,392
To remove every out-of-stock line, use removeIf.

36
00:02:53,392 --> 00:02:54,053
One pass.

37
00:02:54,503 --> 00:02:57,815
The loop version calls remove inside a for-each.

38
00:02:57,815 --> 00:03:06,855
It shifts the tail on every removal, which is quadratic, and it breaks the iterator with a ConcurrentModificationException besides.

39
00:03:07,305 --> 00:03:13,257
removeIf marks the doomed indices in a bit set, then compacts the array in a single copy.

40
00:03:14,157 --> 00:03:14,954
Three traps.

41
00:03:14,954 --> 00:03:19,736
First, subList returns a live view that holds a reference to its parent.

42
00:03:19,736 --> 00:03:25,848
Store ten elements from a million-element list, and the whole backing array stays reachable.

43
00:03:25,848 --> 00:03:29,567
And any structural change to the parent breaks the view.

44
00:03:29,567 --> 00:03:34,018
Treat subList as short-lived, and copy it if it escapes the method.

45
00:03:34,468 --> 00:03:36,540
Second, the remove overload.

46
00:03:36,540 --> 00:03:42,460
On a list of Integer, remove with an int literal removes by index, not by value.

47
00:03:42,460 --> 00:03:45,346
To remove the value, box it explicitly.

48
00:03:45,346 --> 00:03:49,935
This is the most common List bug in code that stores Integers.

49
00:03:50,385 --> 00:03:53,487
Third, Arrays dot asList isn't an ArrayList.

50
00:03:53,487 --> 00:03:56,166
It's a fixed-size view over the array.

51
00:03:56,166 --> 00:03:58,705
Set works, but add and remove throw.

52
00:03:59,605 --> 00:04:02,540
Here's the whole family in one table.

53
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:05,238
A new ArrayList is a mutable copy.

54
00:04:05,238 --> 00:04:09,523
List dot of and copyOf are immutable and reject nulls.

55
00:04:09,523 --> 00:04:13,569
Stream dot toList is unmodifiable but allows nulls.

56
00:04:13,569 --> 00:04:22,218
Arrays dot asList, unmodifiableList, subList and Java 21's reversed are all views, connected to the original.

57
00:04:22,668 --> 00:04:25,592
So from an accessor, return List dot copyOf.

58
00:04:25,592 --> 00:04:31,308
A view stops callers writing, but still leaks your later changes into their reference.

59
00:04:32,208 --> 00:04:39,628
Writing your own ArrayList is a standard interview exercise, and the quickest way to understand the real one.

60
00:04:39,628 --> 00:04:42,146
The key points: capacity is not size.

61
00:04:42,146 --> 00:04:43,984
There are two range checks.

62
00:04:43,984 --> 00:04:48,273
Inserting at size is a legal append, but reading at size isn't.

63
00:04:48,273 --> 00:04:53,514
Removal shifts the tail and nulls the vacated slot for the garbage collector.

64
00:04:53,514 --> 00:04:58,960
And grow uses the same one-and-a-half factor, with a fallback for zero capacity.

65
00:04:59,860 --> 00:05:05,417
Here's what separates I wrote a dynamic array from I understand the JDK class.

66
00:05:05,417 --> 00:05:11,401
The real one implements List and RandomAccess, so the whole ecosystem works with it.

67
00:05:11,851 --> 00:05:15,329
Its iterators are fail-fast, using modCount.

68
00:05:15,779 --> 00:05:21,880
It has subList views, bidirectional list iterators, and a spliterator for parallel streams.

69
00:05:22,330 --> 00:05:25,658
removeIf uses a bit set for a single-pass removal.

70
00:05:26,108 --> 00:05:37,052
And a nice detail: elementData is transient, with a hand-written writeObject that serializes only the elements in use, not the empty tail of the array.

71
00:05:37,952 --> 00:05:39,152
Production rules.

72
00:05:39,152 --> 00:05:41,835
Size the list when you know the count.

73
00:05:41,835 --> 00:05:45,224
Default to ArrayList, and ArrayDeque for queues.

74
00:05:45,224 --> 00:05:47,837
Use removeIf for conditional removal.

75
00:05:47,837 --> 00:05:50,520
Return List dot copyOf from accessors.

76
00:05:50,520 --> 00:05:52,285
Prefer stream dot toList.

77
00:05:52,285 --> 00:05:54,827
And trim lists that shrank for good.

78
00:05:55,277 --> 00:05:56,839
Never store a subList.

79
00:05:56,839 --> 00:06:03,372
Never call remove with an int on a list of Integer without deciding which overload you mean.

80
00:06:03,372 --> 00:06:06,426
Never index a LinkedList in a counted loop.

81
00:06:06,426 --> 00:06:11,823
Don't treat Arrays dot asList as mutable, or unmodifiableList as a snapshot.

82
00:06:11,823 --> 00:06:16,226
And never share a list across threads without synchronisation.

83
00:06:17,126 --> 00:06:17,808
To recap.

84
00:06:17,808 --> 00:06:23,036
Contiguous memory beats scattered nodes, so use ArrayList by default.

85
00:06:23,036 --> 00:06:28,265
It grows by one and a half times, so size it when you know the count.

86
00:06:28,265 --> 00:06:31,977
arraycopy makes O of n inserts cheap in practice.

87
00:06:31,977 --> 00:06:37,585
And subList, Arrays dot asList and unmodifiableList are views, not copies.

88
00:06:38,035 --> 00:06:41,159
Next is chapter nineteen, HashMap internals.

89
00:06:41,159 --> 00:06:42,579
Thanks for watching.
